            .docGeneral: "hop — the little word for a quick, effortless move. one click — and everything you need is here:\n\n• timer — presets, work-rest cycles, time set by mouse or keyboard.\n\n• awake — the Mac keeps running and never dozes off, even with the lid closed\n\n• monitor — live system stats with custom thresholds.\n\n• clipboard — copy history with instant paste.\n\n• converter — compresses and converts images, PDFs, video, audio and documents in batches.\n\n• windows — snaps windows to halves, quarters and thirds of the screen.\n\n• time tracker — a flat list of tasks with a running all-time total; start, stop, and edit the time.\n\n• to-dos — a simple checklist; completed items sink to the bottom.\n\n• header — the icons up top switch between spaces (up to four, arranged in settings); {sym:gearshape} the gear opens settings, ⏻ quits the app.\n\n• internet — one-tap network speed via macOS's own networkQuality (Apple CDN servers).\n\n• hidden modules — torrents, the color eyedropper, text recognition, file archives and the keyboard lock stay off until you ask for them: settings → modules & tabs.\n\n• permissions — a tab of their own in this window: what each one is for, whether macOS has granted it, and what Hop never does. Nothing is asked at launch.\n\n• menu bar — the asterisk is hop itself; a blinking bell means the timer has finished, and the marks in its corners are explained just below.\n\n• your own ai agent can drive hop — claude code, codex or any script. ask it to add a task with a reminder, start a timer, track time, lock the keyboard or snap a window, and it writes a small json file that hop performs at once. hop also publishes what it is doing to a second file, so the agent can read the state before it decides anything. the files sit next to hop's data, and hop.dev's docs/automation.md describes them.\n\n• siri and shortcuts — say «start a timer in hop», «add a task to hop», «lock the keyboard in hop», «keep the mac awake with hop» or «recognize text with hop». nothing to set up: the same five actions also show up in shortcuts and spotlight on their own. apple requires the app's name in a spoken phrase, so a bare «lock the keyboard» goes to the system instead.",
            .docConverterFull: "the converter compresses and converts files in batches: drop them into the window (a whole folder works too — up to 500 files, duplicates are skipped).\n\nimages — JPEG, PNG, HEIC, TIFF, GIF and RAW in; JPEG / PNG / HEIC out, plus modern web formats (AVIF). format, scale and quality are adjustable.\n\nPDF — compression by redrawing pages (~150 dpi): files shrink severalfold, but the pages become pictures — text can no longer be selected or copied. controlled by quality.\n\nvideo — MP4, MOV, M4V in; MP4 or MOV out with original / 1080p / 720p / 540p quality.\n\naudio — MP3, WAV, FLAC, AAC → M4A (AAC). encoding to MP3, MKV or WebM isn't supported by the system.\n\ndocuments — MD, DOCX, DOC, RTF and TXT in; PDF, MD or DOCX out.\n\nthe size estimate is honest: for images and PDFs the first file is trial-converted; for video and audio it's calculated from duration and bitrate.\n\neverything is local, files never leave the Mac. results go to Downloads, next to the original or a custom folder — originals stay untouched.\n\n• file rows — thumbnail, name and each file's own «now → after» size; the circle turns into a green check when done.\n\n• ↗ — the converter row in the panel opens this window; the queue survives closing it.",
            .docWindowsFull: "the window manager snaps the active window into screen zones: halves, quarters, thirds, two-thirds, center and full screen.\n\nthe buttons act on the window of the last active app. on first use macOS asks for the accessibility permission — without it other apps' windows can't be moved.\n\nlayout — in settings: the full 3×6 grid or a compact row of six essential zones.\n\nif a window doesn't fit a zone exactly, that app has a minimum window size macOS won't shrink past.\n\n• glyphs — each button is a mini screen: the filled part is where the window goes. the first row is the essentials — halves, center, full screen.",
            .docTimerFull: "a straightforward countdown timer.\n\n• presets — 5 and 15 are breaks, 25 a pomodoro, 45 an academic hour, 60 an hour, 90 a deep-work cycle. your own presets live in settings.\n\n• dragging — pull the digits with the mouse: over hours it changes hours, over minutes minutes, over seconds seconds.\n\n• typing — click a digit group (hours, minutes or seconds), it lights up yellow, then type: digits slide in from the right.\n\n• restore — a preset tapped mid-countdown saves the running timer; the {sym:arrow.uturn.backward} button brings it back and resumes.\n\n• alert — sound, banner or silent: picked in timer settings.\n\n• stopwatch — the {sym:stopwatch} icon to the right of the presets switches to counting up from zero: start, pause and reset work the same, and the count also shows in the menu bar.\n\n• {sym:playpause} start and pause buttons — the big circles: play starts or resumes (after the finale — restarts), pause holds the countdown, stop resets to the set time.\n\n• ±5 — the «min» capsules add or take five minutes, mid-run too.\n\n• done — the signal fires once; the digits keep blinking as a reminder it rang, right up until you reset. clicking the digits returns the timer to its set time.",
            .docMonitorRows: "cpu — processor load and the hottest spot of the chip. on Apple Silicon the die sensors are shared between CPU and GPU, so a separate GPU temperature is often unavailable.\n\ngpu — graphics load.\n\nmemory — used/total, counted like Activity Monitor.\n\nnetwork — current speed: ↓ download, ↑ upload.",
            .docMonitorRows2: "disk — used/total, percentage and SSD temperature.\n\nbattery — charge and temperature. health — capacity left versus factory (95% = the battery holds 95% of new); cycles — full recharges.\n\npower — adapter wattage plus current charge/draw. the charge figure below the adapter's rating is normal: part of the power runs the Mac itself, the battery gets the rest, and past ~80% the charge current tapers on its own to protect the battery.\n\nuptime — how long the Mac has run since the last reboot.",
            .docMonitorColors: "value colors: white or green — fine, yellow — heavy load, red — critical. thresholds for temperature, load, disk and battery are set in settings, along with °C or °F.\n\ngraphs — the switch in monitor settings turns rows into live charts: a filled area under each row shows how the value changed over the chosen time window.",
            .docAwakeFull: "awake keeps the Mac running: it won't fall asleep, so downloads, builds and long tasks never get interrupted. pick a duration or ∞.\n\nwhen it helps — a long download or build; agents and scripts that must not be interrupted; watching a movie without nudging the mouse; running with the lid closed next to an external display.\n\ndisplay stays on — also keeps the screen lit, not just the system.\n\nrun with lid closed — close the lid without shutting the Mac down: the screen goes dark, everything keeps running (asks for an admin password).\n\nthe yellow moon and the dot on the menu bar icon show it's active. turn it off and normal sleep returns.\n\n• {sym:lid} lid icon — the rightmost button in the row: click enables closed-lid mode (asks an admin password; turns sleep-block on if it was off), click again turns it off. amber = on.",
            .docClipboardFull: "the clipboard keeps what you copy: text, links and file paths.\n\n• bring back — click a row to put it on the clipboard again; the copy button copies, the insert button pastes straight into the active app.\n\n• files — a copied file is stored as its full path; clicking the row puts the file itself back on the clipboard: paste it in Finder as a file, or as the path in a text field.\n\n• privacy — entries stay on this Mac only; concealed content (password managers) is never stored.\n\n• history size — how many recent entries to keep is set in the clipboard settings.\n\n• {sym:arrow.up.left.and.arrow.down.right} expand — the arrows next to the header stretch the list to 20 entries and shrink it back to 5.\n\n• clear — wipes the whole history at once.",
            .docTorrentFull: "a lightweight torrent client right in the panel, enabled separately because it needs a download engine. turning it on fetches that engine once (~26 MB), installed only after its signature checks out.\n\n• adding — drop a .torrent onto the card, paste a magnet link, or open a .torrent from disk. make Hop the default for .torrent files and magnet links and they open here straight from the browser or Finder.\n\n• choosing files — the add sheet lists what's inside; tick the files you want (select or deselect all at once) and confirm. a multi-file torrent expands into a per-file list you can toggle live, each with its own progress.\n\n• {sym:arrow.down.circle} downloading — the row shows percent, size, speed and eta; the download glyph lights up only while bytes are actually arriving. {sym:pause.fill} pause and resume any time.\n\n• {sym:checkmark.circle.fill} done and seeding — a finished torrent keeps seeding (sharing) to others; pause the row to stop, resume to share again.\n\n• stop at ratio 1.0 — off by default; turn it on in settings to stop seeding automatically once you've uploaded as much as you downloaded.\n\n• speed limits — cap download and upload rates (KB/s) in settings; 0 means unlimited. switch the fields between KB/s and MB/s.\n\n• disk space — before a download starts, the free space on the target volume is checked against the selected files; it won't begin if they don't fit.\n\n• where files land — the download folder (Downloads by default, changeable in settings). single-file torrents go straight in; multi-file ones get their own subfolder named after the torrent. {sym:folder} on a finished row reveals it in Finder.\n\n• {sym:xmark} removing — delete the torrent but keep the files on disk, or delete it together with the downloaded files.",
