            .colorLabel: "color picker", .colorPick: "pick", .colorPicking: "picking…",
            .colorEmpty: "no colors yet", .hkColor: "eyedropper",
            .docColorFull: "the eyedropper for colors on screen: press the shortcut (or the pipette icon) and macOS's own loupe appears — click any pixel and the color joins the list below, already on the clipboard.\n\n• {sym:eyedropper} picking — the loupe magnifies whatever is under the cursor, so a single pixel is easy to hit. escape cancels and copies nothing. no screen-recording permission is involved: the system loupe hands back one color and nothing else.\n\n• three notations at once — every color in the list shows hex, rgb and hsl side by side, and each one is a button: click hex to copy hex, click rgb to copy rgb. the list keeps the order the colors were picked in and never reshuffles under the cursor.\n\n• the list is settings — how many colors to keep and how many rows to show before scrolling live in settings → other modules, the same pair the clipboard has.\n\n• they are clipboard entries too — a picked color sits in the clipboard history as well, searchable there like anything else you copied.",
            .ocrLabel: "text recognition", .ocrRead: "select on screen", .ocrHint: "text or qr code",
            .ocrSelecting: "select an area…", .ocrReading: "reading…", .ocrCopied: "copied",
            .ocrNothing: "nothing to read", .ocrNeedsPermission: "needs screen recording", .ocrOpenSettings: "open settings",
            .ocrFailed: "didn't work",
            .docOcrFull: "text and qr codes out of the screen or out of a picture: what is recognized appears in a window you can read, edit and copy from — and it goes into the clipboard history at the same time.\n\n• {sym:text.viewfinder} two ways in — \"select on screen\" hands you macOS's own crosshair to frame an area; the window also takes a picture dropped onto it or pasted with ⌘V. printed and rendered text of any kind works: a paused video, a protected pdf, a dialog that will not let you select, a frame from a game. qr codes and barcodes are read in the same pass.\n\n• the result is visible — a window shows the text instead of quietly filing it away. edit it, select a line, or use the copy button; the text is already in the clipboard history either way, searchable there later.\n\n• line breaks are kept — a table or a code snippet stays readable instead of collapsing into one run-on line.\n\n• recognition — Apple's own Vision, entirely on this Mac and offline; nothing is uploaded anywhere. it reads your app language plus english.\n\n• screen recording — macOS asks for it once, because framing an area means reading pixels; a dropped picture needs no permission at all. escape cancels a selection and copies nothing.\n\n• languages — nothing to choose: recognition works out the writing system itself, so a page in japanese, chinese, korean, russian, arabic or several of them at once is read as it is.",
            .archiveLabel: "file archives", .archiveDrop: "drop an archive to unpack", .archiveDropHint: "files or folders — packed instead",
            .archiveUnpacking: "unpacking…", .archivePacking: "packing…", .archiveUnpacked: "unpacked",
            .archivePacked: "packed", .archiveGettingHelper: "getting helper", .archiveHelperFailed: "helper unavailable",
            .archiveEmpty: "nothing inside", .archiveFailed: "didn't work",
            .docArchiveFull: "packing and unpacking by drag & drop — and nothing starts on its own: what you add waits until you press the button.\n\n• {sym:archivebox} the window — the module's row opens it, and that is where you drop things: a panel closes the moment a drag starts, so it could never hold a drop target. ⌘V works too, several files at once — copy them in Finder and paste them here.\n\n• the list and the button — everything you add is listed, and nothing happens until you press unpack (or pack). archives in the list are unpacked; anything else is packed into ONE archive, so a mixed set packs.\n\n• where it lands — the Desktop by default, because a result has to appear where you are already looking. next to the original, or any folder you choose, is one click away in the same window.\n\n• formats — zip, rar, 7z, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz and gz. rar and 7z fetch the 7-Zip helper (~6 MB) the first time one turns up, installed only after its signature checks out. Hop unpacks rar but never creates it: the format is proprietary.\n\n• packing format — zip, tar.gz or 7z, offered only when the list is going to be packed: an archive being opened comes out as whatever it already is. an existing name is never overwritten; the new one is numbered the way Finder does it, and several loose files come out inside one folder named after the archive.\n\n• opening from Finder — only rar can be assigned to Hop in settings → other modules, and never while an Apple app already owns it. Finder-opened archives unpack beside themselves, regardless of the window's destination setting. A successful double-click stays out of the way; the archive window opens only if the job fails. Earlier associations can be returned to Archive Utility from the same settings section.",
            .convCanDocuments: "documents", .convModeLabel: "mode", .convModeCompress: "compress",
            .convDocNote: "word layout like columns and footnotes is simplified; pdf → md extracts text with headings, not layout",
            .docConverterDocs: "documents — markdown, word files and pdfs turned into one another, in the same window and the same batch.\n\n• md → pdf — laid out by Hop itself: headings, lists, quotes, code blocks and links, on A4 pages with proper margins. the markdown engine is ours, so the result looks deliberate rather than like a browser printout.\n\n• word → pdf or md — .docx, .doc and .rtf: text, headings, emphasis, lists, simple tables and images come across. columns, footnotes and headers do not: those are layout, and the conversion is honest about losing them.\n\n• pdf → md — switch the pdf group to md. a pdf stores layout rather than structure, so this is text EXTRACTION with heading guesses: a text-first document comes out well, a magazine spread comes out as prose. a scanned page with no text layer is read with Vision, the same engine the text recognition module uses.\n\n• md → docx — for when a document has to arrive in a shape Word will open.\n\n• offline — everything is native: no converter service, no bundled office suite, nothing to download.",
            .keylockLabel: "keyboard lock", .keylockStart: "lock",
